The culprit is finding `crime_00022`, “Sync I/O in Hot Path.” Its evidence is:

> "`existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)"  
> "`statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)"  
> "`readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)"  
> "lines: 12, 13, 15"  
> "swap for the async variant (`readFile`, `writeFile`, `exec`, …) and `await` it"

The blocking methods are `existsSync`, `statSync`, and `readFileSync`. Each blocks the Node event loop per request.

The smallest migration is to make the internal `loadPlanSchema` helper asynchronous, use `node:fs/promises`, and await it from the already-async public handler:

```ts
import { readFile, stat } from "node:fs/promises";

async function loadPlanSchema(path: string) {
  try {
    const fileStat = await stat(path);
    if (!fileStat.isFile()) return null;

    return JSON.parse(await readFile(path, "utf8"));
  } catch (error) {
    if (
      error instanceof Error &&
      "code" in error &&
      error.code === "ENOENT"
    ) {
      return null;
    }
    throw error;
  }
}

// Inside the public async handler:
const schema = await loadPlanSchema(schemaPath);
```

This removes `existsSync` entirely—checking existence before reading is redundant and race-prone—and replaces `statSync` and `readFileSync` with their asynchronous counterparts. The externally visible handler signature remains unchanged if it already returns a `Promise`, as production HTTP handlers typically do.

If `loadPlanSchema` itself is exported with a synchronous return type, that exact signature cannot be preserved while making the I/O genuinely asynchronous; retain the public handler signature and keep the newly async loader private instead.
